Tracing the actual function: _executeInstantRedemption decodes params from redeemData, redeems the PT, then executes
  leg 1 (sUSDe→sDAI) with limit: 0, unwraps sDAI→DAI, and then either (a) trades DAI→asset with limit:
  params.minPurchaseAmount, or (b) if asset == DAI, explicitly reverts with SlippageTooHigh(daiAmount,
  params.minPurchaseAmount) when params.minPurchaseAmount > daiAmount. So the composed flow is not unguarded: leg 1's
  zero limit is bounded end-to-end by a final-output check on the cumulative proceeds. Any value extracted by
  sandwiching the hardcoded Curve pool shows up as a smaller sDAIAmount → smaller daiAmount → smaller final output,
  which trips the final-leg limit / the explicit revert. That is exactly what the in-code comment asserts, and the code
  matches the comment.

  Therefore the exploit does not turn on the missing first-leg limit at all — it turns entirely on the premise that an
  attacker gets to author redeemData and set minPurchaseAmount = 0 for someone else's position. Nothing in this file
  establishes that. _executeInstantRedemption is internal; the calling path that accepts redeemData (the vault's
  redeem/exit entry point and whatever authorization it applies to msg.sender vs. the position owner) is not shown. The
  claim simply asserts "approved spender / keeper / automated redemption" supplies it. An approved spender or keeper
  acting on a position is a delegated/trusted role acting within its documented permissions unless the candidate names
  the concrete unprivileged entry point and the missing check — it does neither, and the code that would prove or
  disprove it is absent from the input.

  Additionally, _executeTrade routes through the trading-module abstraction, whose own configured limits are not shown
  here, so I cannot even confirm a zero-limit single-leg trade would be accepted unbounded.

  Both independent grounds — the end-to-end slippage guard on cumulative proceeds, and the unproven/privileged nature of
  the "someone else supplies redeemData" premise — mean the claimed unprivileged exploit is not demonstrated on the
  actual control flow shown. Under the tie-break rule this must be refuted.

  CONSTRAINT|class=C15|An unguarded intermediate hop in a multi-leg swap is not a finding when a downstream check bounds
  the cumulative final output, unless the candidate identifies the concrete unprivileged entry point through which a
  non-owner supplies that downstream guard parameter and shows the authorization check is absent in the code presented.
  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/staking/PendlePT_sUSDe.sol:_executeInstantRedemption:30|class=C15|The zero-limit first leg is
  bounded end-to-end because all proceeds flow into the final leg's params.minPurchaseAmount limit (or the explicit
  SlippageTooHigh revert on the DAI path), and the only way to defeat that guard — a non-owner authoring redeemData —
  depends on caller-authorization code not present in the input and describes a delegated/trusted role rather than a
  demonstrated unprivileged path.
